{% extends "pages/_example.jinja" %}
{% import "_api.jinja" as api %}
{% set example_slug = 'concurrent-jobs' %}
{% set example_facts = [
  ('Level', 'Intermediate'),
  ('Needs', 'Python ' ~ python_floor ~ ' or later; nothing else, as the interpreter stands in for three checks'),
  ('Install', 'Cuprum ' ~ cuprum_pypi ~ ' from PyPI; see Getting started'),
  ('Tested', cuprum_verified_on ~ ' against Cuprum ' ~ cuprum_pypi ~ ' on Linux, Python ' ~ python_floor ~ '; both runs repeated eight times with identical output'),
  ('Uses', 'run_concurrent_sync, ConcurrentConfig (concurrency, fail_fast), ConcurrentResult'),
] %}

{% block problem %}
<p>
  Three independent checks — lint, type checking, and a documentation
  build — have no reason to wait for each other. Run them side by side, but
  not all at once on a small machine, and afterwards know which failed and
  what each printed.
</p>
<p>
  {{ api.ref('run_concurrent_sync') }} runs commands concurrently and
  returns a {{ api.ref('ConcurrentResult') }};
  {{ api.ref('ConcurrentConfig') }}<code>.concurrency</code> bounds how
  many children run at once. In async code, {{ api.ref('run_concurrent') }}
  is the same call awaited.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block code %}
{% call ui.code_panel('checks.py', 'Complete', foot=[verified]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import (
    ConcurrentConfig,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    run_concurrent_sync,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(PYTHON, name="jobs")
python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)


def job(name: str, seconds: float, code: int = 0):
    script = (
        f"import sys, time; time.sleep({seconds}); "
        f"print('{name}'); sys.exit({code})"
    )
    return python("-c", script)


jobs = [job("lint", 0.3), job("types", 0.1, code=2), job("docs", 0.2)]

with scoped(catalogue=CATALOGUE):
    result = run_concurrent_sync(*jobs, config=ConcurrentConfig(concurrency=2))

print(f"ok={result.ok}")
print(f"failures={result.failures}")
for index, item in zip(result.submission_indices, result.results):
    print(index, item.exit_code, item.stdout.strip())
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
{% endblock %}

{% block output %}
<div class="cu-pair">
  {% call ui.output('Expected output', 'stdout') %}
ok=False
failures=(1,)
0 0 lint
1 2 types
2 0 docs
  {% endcall %}
  <div class="cu-prose">
    <p>
      Every check ran to completion, two at a time, and the results come back
      in submission order whatever order they finished in: the type check
      finished first but is listed second. <code>failures</code> holds the
      position of each result that exited non-zero, so the run as a whole is
      not <code>ok</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block failure %}
<div class="cu-prose">
  <p>
    Collecting every result is the default. With <code>fail_fast=True</code>,
    the first non-zero exit cancels everything still running. Here the type
    check fails at once, and the two thirty-second checks are stopped.
  </p>
</div>
<div class="cu-pair mt-8">
{% call ui.code_panel('checks_fail_fast.py', 'Failure path', foot=[verified], flat=true) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import (
    ConcurrentConfig,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    run_concurrent_sync,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(PYTHON, name="jobs")
python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)


def job(name: str, seconds: float, code: int = 0):
    script = (
        f"import sys, time; time.sleep({seconds}); "
        f"print('{name}'); sys.exit({code})"
    )
    return python("-c", script)


jobs = [job("types", 0.1, code=2), job("lint", 30), job("docs", 30)]

config = ConcurrentConfig(concurrency=3, fail_fast=True)
with scoped(catalogue=CATALOGUE):
    result = run_concurrent_sync(*jobs, config=config)

print(f"ok={result.ok}")
print(f"returned {len(result.results)} of {len(jobs)} results")
print(f"submission_indices={result.submission_indices}")
print(f"failure_submission_indices={result.failure_submission_indices}")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  {% call ui.output('Observed output', 'stdout') %}
ok=False
returned 1 of 3 results
submission_indices=(0,)
failure_submission_indices=(0,)
  {% endcall %}
</div>
<div class="cu-prose mt-10">
  <p>
    The run returned in a fraction of a second, with one result: the
    cancelled commands have none. Because a result's position no longer
    matches its command's, map back with <code>submission_indices</code> and
    <code>failure_submission_indices</code> rather than trusting positions. A
    command that had already finished before the cancellation still appears.
  </p>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block policy %}
<p>
  The catalogue lists only this interpreter, and <code>scoped(catalogue=...)</code>
  makes it the allowlist for every command submitted inside the block. Each
  command is checked before it is spawned, so a forbidden one fails with
  {{ api.ref('ForbiddenProgramError') }} rather than running.
</p>
{% endblock %}
